WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.283
Some .

2
00:00:01.283 --> 00:00:08.662
NET applications can avoid password-bearing connection strings by using managed identities or Entra authentication.

3
00:00:08.662 --> 00:00:19.056
These patterns can reduce the need to distribute database usernames and passwords through application configuration, deployment variables, and developer machines.

4
00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:23.933
Passwordless connection patterns do not remove the need for security design.

5
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.654
The application identity still needs database permissions.

6
00:00:27.654 --> 00:00:31.761
The hosting platform still needs correct identity configuration.

7
00:00:31.761 --> 00:00:34.135
The database still needs role design.

8
00:00:34.135 --> 00:00:40.102
Operators still need monitoring, rotation, and incident response for the identities involved.

9
00:00:40.102 --> 00:00:46.454
Managed identity can be useful because the platform can issue and manage identity for the workload.

10
00:00:46.454 --> 00:00:52.870
The application can authenticate to supported services without storing a reusable database password.

11
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:59.800
That reduces one class of secret handling, but the identity remains powerful and should be scoped carefully.

12
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.986
Local development and cloud-hosted production may use different identity sources while sharing application code.

13
00:01:06.986 --> 00:01:14.044
Developers may use local tooling or development identities, while production uses a managed workload identity.

14
00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:18.985
The configuration should make those differences explicit and hard to confuse.

15
00:01:18.985 --> 00:01:23.797
Database users, roles, and permissions still need deliberate configuration.

16
00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:29.187
A passwordless connection using an overly broad database role is still overly broad.

17
00:01:29.187 --> 00:01:34.127
Least privilege, separation of duties, and environment isolation still apply.

18
00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:45.292
Passwordless approaches should be reviewed for supported providers, hosting environment, developer tooling, migration workflows, operational ownership, and fallback behavior.

19
00:01:45.292 --> 00:01:50.168
The best design is the one the team can operate reliably and review clearly.
